13. IMPORTANT DECLARATION-ORDER BUG WE ENCOUNTERED
============================================================

During the preview change, we initially referenced:

selectedPreviewPosition

before its declaration.

This produced:

Runtime ReferenceError

Cannot access 'selectedPreviewPosition' before initialization

The problem was not the formula itself.

The problem was JavaScript const declaration order.

The variable had originally been declared later in the component around the
Entry Details calculation area.

We moved:

const selectedPreviewPosition =
  isPartialExit
    ? manualOpenPositions.find(
        (position) =>
          position.contractKey ===
          selectedPositionContractKey
      )
    : undefined;

to ABOVE the first place where it is referenced.

Correct structure is now:

const selectedPreviewPosition =
  isPartialExit
    ? manualOpenPositions.find(
        (position) =>
          position.contractKey ===
          selectedPositionContractKey
      )
    : undefined;

const previewEntryPriceForExit =
  Number(selectedPreviewPosition?.entryPrice ?? 0);

const previewEntryQuantityForExit =
  Number(selectedPreviewPosition?.quantity ?? 0);

...

Then later:

const selectedEntryDate = ...

const selectedEntryTime = ...

const selectedEntryQuantity = ...

const selectedEntryPrice = ...

This fixed the runtime error.

IMPORTANT:
There must be only ONE declaration of:

const selectedPreviewPosition

Do not reintroduce a second declaration later.

